button to \"Request a New Reset Link\"\n    And prevents new password form submission","starter_code":"Feature: Password Reset\n  Scenario: Expired reset token handling\n    # TODO: Write Given, When, Then steps","title":"Exercise 1: Gherkin Acceptance Criteria Drafting"},{"scenario":"Given a baseline 2-point story: \"Add a copy button to code blocks\", estimate the story points for: \"Implement OAuth2 Google Sign-in with database user creation and session token generation\". Justify using complexity, effort, and risk.","solution":"// Estimated Points: 5 or 8 points\n// Rationale:\n// Complexity: High (integrating external OAuth2 protocol, handling callback redirects, error edge cases).\n// Effort: Medium-High (configuring Google Cloud credentials, writing user lookups/inserts, generating JWT tokens).\n// Risk/Uncertainty: High (security token management, third-party network failure).\n// Roughly 3x to 4x the effort of a simple UI copy button.","starter_code":"// Estimated Points: ?\n// Rationale:","title":"Exercise 2: Story Point Estimation Exercise"}],"objectives":["Master Scrum ceremonies (Sprint Planning, Daily Standup, Sprint Review, Retrospective).","Write clear User Stories adhering to INVEST criteria and define comprehensive Definitions of Done (DoD).","Estimate story points using Planning Poker, Fibonacci scale, and track team velocity.","Manage collaborative GitHub Projects / Jira boards, branch naming conventions, and Pull Request engineering SLAs."],"outcome":"Practical readiness for professional software team workflows, agile delivery, and cross-functional collaboration.","project":"Simulate 2-Week Agile Sprint with GitHub Projects Board, User Stories, PR Reviews & Burndown Tracking","quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The Product Owner is solely accountable for maximizing the value of the product and managing the Product Backlog, including content, ordering, and transparency.","id":1,"options":["The Scrum Master","The Product Owner","The Lead Architect","The Engineering Manager"],"question":"Who is responsible for prioritizing and maintaining the items in the Product Backlog in Scrum?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"INVEST stands for: Independent (can be developed and released without tight coupling to other stories), Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, and Testable.","id":2,"options":["Immediate","Independent","Iterative","Intricate"],"question":"What does the \"I\" stand for in the INVEST criteria for agile user stories?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The Daily Standup focuses on inspect-and-adapt progress toward the Sprint Goal: what was done yesterday, what will be done today, and any impediments or blockers.","id":3,"options":["How many hours did I log? Did I finish my tickets? Who is responsible for blockers?","What did I accomplish yesterday? What do I plan to accomplish today? Are there any blockers or impediments in my way?","What bugs did I introduce? When will the sprint end? Who approved my PR?","Did the build pass? What is the current velocity? What is our budget?"],"question":"During a Daily Standup meeting, what are the three standard questions each team member answers?"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Sprint Review focuses on the PRODUCT (demonstrating completed work to stakeholders and gathering feedback). Sprint Retrospective focuses on the PROCESS (how the team collaborated and what can be improved in the next sprint).","id":4,"options":["The Sprint Review inspects the product increment with stakeholders; the Retrospective inspects team process, tools, and relationships to identify continuous improvements","Sprint Review is only for managers; Retrospective is only for QA engineers","Sprint Review takes place at the beginning of the sprint; Retrospective takes place at mid-sprint","There is no difference; they are alternative names for the same ceremony"],"question":"What is the primary difference between the Sprint Review and the Sprint Retrospective?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The Definition of Done is a formal agreement on the quality standards required for the product increment (e.g. code written, unit tested, reviewed, documented, and passing CI/CD) before it can be considered done.","id":5,"options":["The date when the developer pushes their branch to GitHub","A shared, formal checklist of quality criteria that an increment must satisfy before being considered complete and releasable","When the customer approves the initial wireframe mockup","When all story points for the quarter have been assigned"],"question":"What is the definition of \"Definition of Done\" (DoD)?"}],"resources":[{"description":"The definitive reference guide written by Scrum co-creators Ken Schwaber and Jeff Sutherland.","name":"The Official Scrum Guide (2020)","url":"https://scrumguides.org/scrum-guide.html"},{"description":"Comprehensive tutorials on Scrum, Kanban, user stories, and agile project delivery.","name":"Atlassian Agile Coach","url":"https://www.atlassian.com/agile"},{"description":"Google standards for reviewing code, giving constructive feedback, and maintaining velocity.","name":"Google Engineering Practices: Code Review","url":"https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/"}],"syllabus":[{"code_snippet":"// Scrum Sprint Cycle Summary\n// 1. Sprint Planning (Day 1): Define Sprint Goal & commit to Backlog items\n// 2. Daily Standup (15 min daily): \n//    - What did I complete yesterday?\n//    - What will I complete today?\n//    - Any blockers impeding progress?\n// 3. Sprint Review (Last Day): Live product demo to stakeholders\n// 4. Sprint Retrospective (Last Day): Team reflection: What went well? What to improve?","deep_dive":"Scrum is an iterative framework built on empiricism. Sprints are fixed-length timeboxes (typically 2 weeks). The Product Owner owns WHAT to build; Developers own HOW to build it; the Scrum Master facilitates the process.","key_concepts":["Three Roles (PO, Scrum Master, Developers)","Four Ceremonies","Three Artifacts (Product Backlog, Sprint Backlog, Increment)","Empiricism (Transparency, Inspection, Adaptation)"],"module_title":"Module 1: The Scrum Framework Architecture"},{"code_snippet":"# Production User Story Template\nTitle: Candidate PDF Resume Upload & Instant ATS Parsing\n\nAs a registered job seeker,\nI want to upload my resume PDF in the CareerSense AI analyzer,\nSo that I receive an immediate breakdown of my skills and ATS score.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\nScenario: Successful PDF Parse\n  Given I am logged into my CareerSense dashboard\n  When I drag and drop a valid PDF under 5MB\n  Then the system displays an animated analysis pipeline\n  And persists parsed skills to my MySQL candidate profile\n  And redirects to the Resume Intelligence score card\n\nDefinition of Done (DoD):\n  [x] Unit tests pass with >= 85% coverage\n  [x] Pull request reviewed and approved by peer\n  [x] Linting and build pipeline pass green\n  [x] Verified in staging environment","deep_dive":"User stories must be INVEST: Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, and Testable. Acceptance criteria define boundary conditions using Given-When-Then syntax.","key_concepts":["User Story Format (As a... I want... So that...)","INVEST Quality Checklist","Acceptance Criteria (Gherkin syntax)","Definition of Done (DoD)"],"module_title":"Module 2: Writing Production User Stories (INVEST)"},{"code_snippet":"// Velocity Calculation Example\n// Sprint 1 Completed Points: 28\n// Sprint 2 Completed Points: 32\n// Sprint 3 Completed Points: 30\n// Average Team Velocity = (28 + 32 + 30) / 3 = 30 points / sprint\n// Planned Backlog for Sprint 4 should not exceed 30 story points.","deep_dive":"Story points estimate relative effort, complexity, and uncertainty\u2014not hours. A 5-point story is roughly 2.5x the effort of a 2-point story regardless of who develops it.","key_concepts":["Story Points vs Hours","Fibonacci Scale (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13)","Planning Poker","Burndown Charts & Velocity Tracking"],"module_title":"Module 3: Relative Estimation & Sprint Velocity"},{"code_snippet":"# Pull Request Template (.github/pull_request_template.md)\n## Description\nBrief summary of changes made in this branch.\n\n## Linked Issue\nFixes #42 (Jira/GitHub issue number)\n\n## Type of Change\n- [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change fixing an issue)\n- [ ] New feature (non-breaking change adding functionality)\n\n## Checklist\n- [ ] Code follows project styling guidelines\n- [ ] Added unit tests covering all edge cases\n- [ ] No regression warnings or console errors","deep_dive":"Engineering teams enforce clear PR standards: small diffs (< 400 lines), descriptive PR descriptions, passing automated CI tests, and peer approvals before merging to main.","key_concepts":["Trunk-Based / Feature Branching","PR Description Templates","Constructive Code Review SLAs","Automated CI Quality Gates"],"module_title":"Module 4: Professional Git PR Collaboration & Code Review SLAs"}],"title":"Agile Software Engineering & Scrum Sprint Delivery","topics":["Scrum Framework Roles, Ceremonies & Artifacts","User Stories, INVEST Criteria & Definition of Done","Relative Estimation (Fibonacci) & Sprint Velocity Metrics","Engineering SLAs, Git PR Review Standards & CI Workflows"]},"duration_weeks":"Week 1 (Sprint)","expected_outcome":"Demonstrated practical competency in Agile with functional, reviewed portfolio code.","id":512,"order_index":1,"phase_number":1,"practice_project":"Build a practical Agile mini-project demonstrating core capabilities required for Full Stack Developer.","priority":"MEDIUM","progress":0,"resource_categories":["Official Agile Documentation","Modern Agile Guide for Developers","Community Hands-on Exercises"],"roadmap_id":39,"skill_name":"Agile","status":"Not Started","tasks":[{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":1,"description":"Study core architecture, terminology, and foundational concepts of Agile.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Core Fundamentals & Concept Overview","title":"Day 1: Core Fundamentals & Concept Overview","week":1},{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":2,"description":"Configure local development environment, tooling, and run first working Agile example.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Environment Setup & Hello-World","title":"Day 2: Environment Setup & Hello-World","week":1},{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":3,"description":"Practice key syntax patterns, methods, and configurations in Agile.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Syntax & Essential APIs","title":"Day 3: Syntax & Essential APIs","week":1},{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":4,"description":"Complete guided coding exercises implementing Agile Core Architecture & Syntax.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Hands-on Coding Exercises","title":"Day 4: Hands-on Coding Exercises","week":1},{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":5,"description":"Connect Agile with an external library or data source and test functionality.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Mini Integration Lab","title":"Day 5: Mini Integration Lab","week":1}],"title":"Phase 1: Agile Engineering Mastery","topics":["Agile Core Architecture & Syntax","Agile Standard Library & Ecosystem","Design Patterns & Best Practices in Agile","Testing, Debugging & Performance Optimization in Agile"]},{"curriculum":{"exercises":[{"scenario":"Create a production .dockerignore file preventing local dependencies, environment secrets, and git metadata from leaking into the build context.","solution":"# .dockerignore\nnode_modules\nnpm-debug.log\n.git\n.gitignore\n.env\n.env.*\n*.md\ncoverage\ndist","starter_code":"# .dockerignore\n# TODO: add necessary ignore rules","title":"Exercise 1: Create an Optimized .dockerignore"},{"scenario":"Configure a healthcheck for an Express or Flask backend that curls http://localhost:5000/api/health every 15 seconds.","solution":"    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"curl -f http://localhost:5000/api/health || exit 1\"]\n      interval: 15s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 3\n      start_period: 10s","starter_code":"# docker-compose healthcheck snippet\n    healthcheck:\n      # TODO: define test, interval, timeout, retries","title":"Exercise 2: Docker Compose Healthcheck Configuration"}],"objectives":["Understand Linux namespaces, cgroups, and container virtualization versus virtual machines.","Write optimized, secure, multi-stage Dockerfiles with minimal image footprints and non-root users.","Orchestrate multi-service environments (Frontend, API, DB) using Docker Compose with internal networks.","Manage container networking, health checks, environment secrets, and persistent named volumes."],"outcome":"Ability to package and deploy multi-tier web applications in isolated, reproducible Docker containers for any cloud host.","project":"Containerize Full-Stack CareerSense AI Application with Docker Compose, Persistent MySQL & NGINX","quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Multi-stage builds allow you to use large images with SDKs and compilers during the build stage, and then copy only the compiled artifacts into a minimal production runtime image (like alpine), dramatically shrinking image size and attack surface.","id":1,"options":["It runs containers across multiple cloud providers simultaneously","It keeps the final production image lightweight and secure by discarding build tools, compilers, and devDependencies from earlier stages","It enables hot reloading in production environments","It automatically converts JavaScript into native machine code"],"question":"What is the primary benefit of a multi-stage Docker build?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Docker caches each instruction layer. If you copy package.json first and run npm install, Docker reuses that cached layer as long as package.json has not changed, skipping the slow dependency install step on source code edits.","id":2,"options":["npm install fails if other source files are already present","To leverage Docker layer caching: dependencies are only re-installed when package.json changes, making subsequent code builds instant","It is a mandatory syntax requirement of the Dockerfile spec","To prevent git commits from modifying node_modules"],"question":"Why should you COPY package.json and run npm install BEFORE copying the rest of your application source code in a Dockerfile?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Docker Compose creates a custom bridge network where an embedded DNS server automatically maps container and service names (like \"db\" or \"backend\") to their assigned internal container IP addresses.","id":3,"options":["By hardcoding the host computer's localhost:3000 IP address","Using Docker's internal embedded DNS server that automatically resolves peer service names (e.g. http://db:3306)","Through external public DNS servers like 8.8.8.8","Containers cannot communicate over Docker bridge networks"],"question":"How do two containers in the same user-defined Docker Compose bridge network resolve each other's IP addresses?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The writable layer on top of an image is ephemeral and tied to the lifecycle of that specific container. When the container is destroyed, its writable layer is deleted. Persistent data must be mapped to named volumes.","id":4,"options":["The data is automatically saved to the host Desktop folder","The data is permanently lost unless stored in a mounted Docker Volume or Bind Mount","The data is pushed to Docker Hub automatically","Docker saves a zip snapshot in /tmp"],"question":"What happens to data stored inside a container's writable layer when the container is deleted (docker rm)?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Running containers as root poses a severe security risk. If a container breakout vulnerability occurs, the attacker inherits root privileges on the host system. Non-root execution strictly limits attack capabilities.","id":5,"options":["Non-root users execute JavaScript 15% faster","To prevent privilege escalation attacks: if an attacker exploits a web vulnerability to gain a container shell, they will not have root privileges on the host kernel","The Docker daemon rejects containers started by root","Non-root users bypass container memory limits"],"question":"Why is running a container with USER node (non-root) strongly recommended in production?"}],"resources":[{"description":"The official guide to Docker Engine, Dockerfiles, Compose, and CLI tools.","name":"Docker Official Documentation","url":"https://docs.docker.com/"},{"description":"Official recommendations on caching, multi-stage builds, and small images.","name":"Best Practices for Writing Dockerfiles","url":"https://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/dockerfile_best-practices/"},{"description":"Reference manual for docker-compose.yml properties, volumes, and networks.","name":"Docker Compose Specification","url":"https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose-file/"}],"syllabus":[{"code_snippet":"# Inspecting Docker Image Layers and History\ndocker image history node:18-alpine\n\n# Running an interactive lightweight container with memory limit\ndocker run -it --rm --name test-sandbox -m 256m --memory-swap 256m alpine:3.19 sh","deep_dive":"Containers are isolated Linux processes sharing the host kernel via namespaces (which provide isolation) and cgroups (which enforce CPU/memory limits). They boot in milliseconds compared to heavy VMs.","key_concepts":["Containers vs Hypervisor Virtual Machines","Linux Namespaces (PID, NET, MNT)","Control Groups (cgroups)","Union File Systems & Image Layers"],"module_title":"Module 1: Container Architecture & The Docker Engine"},{"code_snippet":"# Multi-Stage Node.js Production Dockerfile\n# Stage 1: Build Dependencies\nFROM node:20-alpine AS builder\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm run build && npm prune --production\n\n# Stage 2: Minimal Production Runner\nFROM node:20-alpine AS runner\nWORKDIR /app\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n\n# Security: Never run containers as root!\nUSER node\n\nCOPY --chown=node:node --from=builder /app/package*.json ./\nCOPY --chown=node:node --from=builder /app/node_modules ./node_modules\nCOPY --chown=node:node --from=builder /app/dist ./dist\n\nEXPOSE 3000\nCMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]","deep_dive":"Multi-stage builds separate the build environment (compilers, devDependencies) from the lean runtime image. Ordering instructions from least frequently changed to most frequently changed maximizes layer cache reuse.","key_concepts":["Multi-Stage Builds","Layer Caching Strategy",".dockerignore Optimization","Non-Root Security Context"],"module_title":"Module 2: Production Multi-Stage Dockerfiles"},{"code_snippet":"# Production Docker Compose Architecture\nversion: '3.8'\n\nservices:\n  db:\n    image: mysql:8.0\n    container_name: careersense-db\n    environment:\n      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: rootsecretpassword\n      MYSQL_DATABASE: careersense_ai\n    volumes:\n      - mysql_data:/var/lib/mysql\n    networks:\n      - internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"mysqladmin\", \"ping\", \"-h\", \"localhost\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  backend:\n    build:\n      context: ./backend\n      dockerfile: Dockerfile\n    container_name: careersense-api\n    depends_on:\n      db:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    environment:\n      DATABASE_URL: mysql+pymysql://root:rootsecretpassword@db:3306/careersense_ai\n    networks:\n      - internal-net\n    ports:\n      - \"5000:5000\"\n\nvolumes:\n  mysql_data:\n\nnetworks:\n  internal-net:\n    driver: bridge","deep_dive":"Docker Compose orchestrates multi-container applications on an isolated bridge network. Containers resolve peer services by service name using embedded DNS (e.g. connecting to mysql:3306).","key_concepts":["docker-compose.yml Syntax","Bridge Networks & Service Discovery","Named Volumes vs Bind Mounts","depends_on with Condition: service_healthy"],"module_title":"Module 3: Multi-Container Orchestration with Docker Compose"},{"code_snippet":"# Managing Docker Volumes & Vulnerability Audits\n# Create and inspect persistent named volume\ndocker volume create db_storage\ndocker volume inspect db_storage\n\n# Scan local image for CVE security vulnerabilities\ndocker scout quickview careersense-api:latest","deep_dive":"Container writable layers are ephemeral; destroying a container deletes all modified files. Named volumes store database files directly on the host Docker storage directory, surviving container lifecycle events.","key_concepts":["Data Persistence across Container Reboots","Host Ports vs Container Ports","Secrets Management","Vulnerability Scanning with Docker Scout"],"module_title":"Module 4: Storage, Networking & Production Security"}],"title":"Docker Containerization & Multi-Container Systems","topics":["Docker Engine Architecture, Namespaces & cgroups","Production Dockerfiles & Multi-Stage Image Builds","Docker Compose Multi-Container Orchestration","Volumes, Networking, Container Healthchecks & Security"]},"duration_weeks":"Week 1 (Sprint)","expected_outcome":"Ability to package and deploy multi-tier web applications in isolated, reproducible Docker containers for any cloud host.","id":513,"order_index":2,"phase_number":2,"practice_project":"Containerize Full-Stack CareerSense AI Application with Docker Compose, Persistent MySQL & NGINX","priority":"HIGH","progress":0,"resource_categories":[{"description":"The official guide to Docker Engine, Dockerfiles, Compose, and CLI tools.","name":"Docker Official Documentation","url":"https://docs.docker.com/"},{"description":"Official recommendations on caching, multi-stage builds, and small images.","name":"Best Practices for Writing Dockerfiles","url":"https://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/dockerfile_best-practices/"},{"description":"Reference manual for docker-compose.yml properties, volumes, and networks.","name":