it is always ready to deploy, but the actual production release requires a manual trigger. In Continuous Deployment, every change that passes tests is deployed automatically.","id":1,"options":["Continuous Delivery is for mobile apps; Continuous Deployment is for web apps","In Continuous Delivery, releasing to production requires manual human approval, whereas in Continuous Deployment every passing build is released automatically without human intervention","Continuous Deployment does not run automated tests","Continuous Delivery uses Jenkins; Continuous Deployment uses GitHub Actions"],"question":"What is the primary difference between Continuous Delivery and Continuous Deployment?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"In GitHub Actions, the needs keyword specifies prerequisite jobs (e.g. needs: [lint, test]). By default, jobs run in parallel unless chained with needs.","id":2,"options":["depends_on","needs","after","requires"],"question":"Which keyword in GitHub Actions workflow syntax specifies that a job must wait for another job to complete successfully before starting?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"GitHub Actions Secrets securely encrypts sensitive values. Secret values are never exposed in logs (automatically masked) and can only be decrypted by runners during workflow execution.","id":3,"options":["In a public config.json file in the repository root","In encrypted GitHub Actions Secrets (Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions)","In commit messages","In the README.md file"],"question":"Where should sensitive cloud credentials (such as AWS keys or database passwords) be stored in a GitHub repository?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Caching the package-lock dependencies avoids downloading hundreds of megabytes over the network on every commit, speeding up CI builds and conserving runner minutes.","id":4,"options":["It prevents npm from charging credit card fees","It reuses downloaded node_modules packages between runs, reducing pipeline execution time from minutes to seconds","It skips executing JavaScript unit tests","It runs tests on older Node.js versions"],"question":"What is the benefit of caching npm dependencies in GitHub Actions using actions/setup-node cache: \"npm\"?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"By default, any step that exits with a non-zero exit code marks the job as failed and immediately terminates further step execution, preventing broken builds or failing tests from proceeding to deployment.","id":5,"options":["The runner continues executing all remaining steps as if nothing happened","The job fails immediately, stops executing subsequent steps, and marks the workflow run as failed","The workflow automatically creates a pull request fixing the error","The repository is deleted"],"question":"What happens if any single step inside a GitHub Actions job fails with an exit code other than 0?"}],"resources":[{"description":"Complete reference manual for GitHub Actions workflow syntax, triggers, and runners.","name":"GitHub Actions Official Documentation","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"description":"Principles and practices of automated software release management by Jez Humble.","name":"Continuous Delivery Handbook","url":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"},{"description":"Security guide on protecting pipeline secrets and dependencies.","name":"OWASP CI/CD Security Top 10","url":"https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-ci-cd-security-risks/"}],"syllabus":[{"code_snippet":"// Typical CI/CD Pipeline Lifecycle:\n// 1. Developer pushes commit to branch 'feature/resume-parser'\n// 2. GitHub Actions fires 'pull_request' trigger:\n//    - Stage 1: ESLint & Flake8 syntax checks\n//    - Stage 2: PyTest & Jest automated test suites\n//    - Stage 3: Build Docker container image\n// 3. PR approved & merged into 'main':\n//    - Stage 4: Push tagged image to Docker Hub / AWS ECR\n//    - Stage 5: Deploy container to production cloud server","deep_dive":"Continuous Integration validates every commit with automated builds and test suites. Continuous Delivery keeps the artifact in a deployable state; Continuous Deployment automatically releases passing artifacts directly to production.","key_concepts":["Continuous Integration vs Delivery vs Deployment","Shift-Left Testing","Trunk-Based Development","Pipeline Stages (Lint -> Test -> Build -> Deploy)"],"module_title":"Module 1: CI/CD Fundamentals & Pipeline Architecture"},{"code_snippet":"# .github/workflows/ci.yml\nname: CareerSense CI Pipeline\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ main ]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [ main ]\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout Repository\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Setup Node.js Environment\n        uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: 20\n          cache: 'npm'\n          cache-dependency-path: frontend/package-lock.json\n\n      - name: Install Frontend Dependencies\n        run: cd frontend && npm ci\n\n      - name: Run Frontend Lint & Build\n        run: cd frontend && npm run build","deep_dive":"Workflows are declared in YAML. Each workflow contains one or more jobs that run in parallel by default on isolated VM runners (e.g. ubuntu-latest). The needs keyword chains jobs sequentially.","key_concepts":[".github/workflows/ YAML files","Events (push, pull_request, workflow_dispatch)","Jobs & Dependency Chaining (needs: ...)","Actions Marketplace & actions/checkout"],"module_title":"Module 2: GitHub Actions Workflow Syntax & Runners"},{"code_snippet":"# Docker Build and Publish Job in GitHub Actions\n  build-and-push:\n    needs: test\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Login to Docker Hub\n        uses: docker/login-action@v3\n        with:\n          username: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_USERNAME }}\n          password: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_TOKEN }}\n\n      - name: Build and Push Docker Image\n        uses: docker/build-push-action@v5\n        with:\n          context: ./backend\n          push: true\n          tags: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_USERNAME }}/careersense-api:latest","deep_dive":"Never store credentials in code or YAML. GitHub Secrets encrypts environment variables (DOCKERHUB_USERNAME, DOCKERHUB_TOKEN). Docker Buildx utilizes GitHub cache layers to speed up builds by 80%.","key_concepts":["Container Registries (Docker Hub / GitHub GHCR)","Semantic Versioning Tags (v1.0.0, sha-...)","GitHub Secrets Vault","Docker Buildx Caching"],"module_title":"Module 3: Docker Registry Publishing & Image Tagging"},{"code_snippet":"# Deploy Step via SSH to Cloud Server\n  deploy:\n    needs: build-and-push\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Execute Remote SSH Deployment\n        uses: appleboy/ssh-action@v1.0.3\n        with:\n          host: ${{ secrets.EC2_HOST }}\n          username: ubuntu\n          key: ${{ secrets.EC2_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY }}\n          script: |\n            cd /opt/careersense\n            docker compose pull backend\n            docker compose up -d --no-deps backend\n            docker system prune -f","deep_dive":"Once a verified container is pushed to the registry, the deployment step SSHs into the production EC2 instance, pulls the latest image, runs database migrations, and performs a graceful rolling restart.","key_concepts":["SSH Remote Dispatch with appleboy/ssh-action","Docker Compose Pull & Up","Zero-Downtime Rolling Restarts","Rollback Strategies on Failure"],"module_title":"Module 4: Automated Cloud Deployment & Zero-Downtime Rollouts"}],"title":"Continuous Integration & Continuous Deployment Pipelines","topics":["CI/CD Core Philosophies & Branching Strategies","GitHub Actions Workflow Syntax, Runners & Triggers","Automated Unit/Integration Testing & Docker Image Registries","Secrets Management, SSH Deployment & Production Release Automation"]},"duration_weeks":"Week 5 (Sprint)","expected_outcome":"A